A||||5||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||Romantic Honeymoon?, aka "Don't go  back to Gorman, CA"||||1||
Z||000000||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||02-17-2001||02:55 AM||||I thought I might share the story of our recent honeymoon with the Forum.  We were married on February 10.  It was a small ceremony that went very smoothly.<P>On the 11th of Feb, we packed up my Ford Ranger and headed south from San Jose to San Diego for our honeymoon.  In preparation for our trip I had a little work done on our 1994 Ranger:  Brakes, timing belt, flush all fluids, water pump, thermostat, shocks, tires, plugs, wires, air filter, camper shell, alpine stereo, new tires, and more.<P>The first four hours were as expected, 300 miles of highway cruising.  We stopped and got a horrible "truck stop" dinner outside of Bakersfield.  My wife's shift at the wheel was over, so I took over for the second leg.  <P>About 45 minutes later we topped the grapevine and the transmission just let go allowing the engine to rev freely.  No warning, transmission serviced less than 1,000 miles before.  Our truck coasted helplessly past a highway patrol car and we took the exit for Gorman, CA.<P>At the stop sign at the end of the ramp, the truck found a last gasp of life and got us into the parking lot at the Mobil station.  I bought some tranny fluid and started to pour it in.  It poured out of the bottom of the truck fast.  We pushed it into a parking spot and went inside.  <P>All of this happened at about 10pm in the middle of a serious rainstorm (3" in 4 hours).  The mechanic was off duty, but at the garage for some unknown reason.  He said that "I have seen those before, it will be $400" without even looking at the truck.  He didn't even know what kind of truck it was.  He went on saying "And then we have the impound fees of about $25 per day.  I don't know when we can get to it, we're pretty busy lately."<P>I felt concerned, so I went outside and called AAA to tow the truck to another station.  The driver of the tow truck suggested we tow it back to San Jose saying "it'll be about $200"  and then left our car at the Gorman "Garage" accross the street from the Econo Lodge.<P>We checked into a room with dizzying visions of a bill for transmission replacement on our minds.  We were on our way to a beach condo in San Diego as we watched the snow fall on us in Gorman.  Would we ever see the place we paid $1000 to rent for the week?  <P>We could not make a long distance call out of the hotel as they did not ALLOW it.  My sprint PCS phone and Ricochet modem were both useless.  The hotel phones would not allow modem connections so we were disconnected from the world.  We tried to make collect calls but our friends were not at home.  We tried to rent a car, but no agency would come out to Gorman to pick us up.  We went to bed fearing that our honeymoon had turned into an episode of the twilight zone.  My new wife was in tears and I could not blame her one bit.<P>The next morning, I walked over to the "Garage" to see when the truck could be repaired and what it would cost.  I went inside to find that the jerk mechanic from the Mobil station was there.  In the driveway was a AAA tow truck dropping off another breakdown.  The owner of the car talked with the jerk mechanic and then went to the hotel to check in.  I was stupified by the obvious relationship between the AAA towyard, Mobil Station, "Garage", and Econolodge.  They wanted to keep people in the "Econo-prison" as there was no other reason to be in Gorman.<P>My wife could not stay at the hotel again, it was pissing her off to the point of panic. We decided to tow the truck back to San Jose rather than let the Jerk Mechanic work on it.  At least we would be out of Gorman.<P>When the truck was towed away, we found out that it would go to another tow yard to wait for the next "double tow" on a flatbed to San Jose.  It was then that we were informed the tow would cost $900 not $200.  While this news sucked, it was clear that the establishment was honest.  The offered to put a new tranny in the truck for $1800 instead.  It was a bit high, but sounded about right to me.  We agreed to have the tranny installed.<P>This shop called Hertz and they brought us a car.  Five hours later, we were in San Diego.  A nine hour drive that took two days.<P>Bad enough for ya?  Well read on, there's more.  <P>The shop said "three days and it will be done" so I asked "can we pick it up next Saturday? (six days later).  They said "no problem".  Then it snowed for two days in Gorman.  They got more than 3ft of snow and had to close down the shop and all of the roads up there.  It was bad enough to be on the News.  The "Grapevine" was just a parking lot filled with abandonded vehicles.  <BR>I called the shop on Wednesday and they said that the truck had been dug out and was just waiting for parts.  They asked me to call back on Friday.<P>I called on Friday and they said "we are just waiting on the parts, call again on Saturday"  I said "Will it be ready on Saturday"<P>They said "Give us a call toworrow morning and we can discuss your situation . . ."<P>Saturday is our last day in the condo.  The truck is exactly half way between San Diego and San Jose, about 225 miles either way.  Our rental car is due back on Saturday.  <P>All of this leaves me wondering what comes next.  And, the $1800 I am spending on the truck transmission comes directly out of my Fiero budget.  Thank god I will likely have only one honeymoon in my life!<P>Oh, we had a good time in San Diego despite all of it.  It's a wonderful place even in the wintertime.||||reg||1
Z||000001||bHooper||02-17-2001||08:11 AM||||dude, that's rough!<P>but that is also, exactly why we are renting a car for the our 2 week honeymoon, this summer. if it breaks, it ain't my problem, other than calling to get a new one delivered!<P>best of luck!<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000002||ChadMan||02-17-2001||08:25 AM||||Man, Lucky, that's terrible.  I hope those scumbags do a good job on the transmission, at least.<P>My wife and I took my father-in-law's Fiero on our honeymoon (Columbus to Phoenix) and never had any trouble except a flat tire.  I don't know if we were smart (for taking the Fiero) or just lucky.||||reg||9
Z||000003||bHooper||02-17-2001||10:33 AM||||i would <B>LOVE</B> to take the Fiero, but i don't have the A/C fixed yet and it certainly won't be painted by then.  besides, with it being 1" lower, we can't get it into the future inlaws "resort" at Lake of the Ozarks.<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000004||LuckyTheyWereEverMade||02-17-2001||12:38 PM||||I strongly recommend a rental car for honeymoons.  <P>We rented a 2000 Mazda 626 with the Hertz "Never Lost" GPS navigator.  It is a great navigational aid that made the rest of our honeymoon trouble free.  Our only regret is that we did not start out in the rental car.  <P>With any luck, we will be back in San Jose by the end of the day.  Keep your fingers crossed . . .||||reg||1
Z||000005||My7Fieros||02-19-2001||07:01 PM||||Ok dude..its been two days since your last post...what happened?!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (its like reading a good book lol)<BR>Oh yeah...I didnt now it snowed in southern California  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by My7Fieros (edited 02-19-2001).]||||reg||1
